er than two  indicates a potential signal  we also looked at delta eight versus  cannabidiol or cbd now remember most of  this is epidiolex in there  and we see a reporting  ratio of greater than 12 in the  respiratory thoracic body system we also  see an increase in the vascular diseases  and musculoskeletal  so when we look at the delta eight cases  descriptively most of them are between  21 and 35 years of age  22 of the cases occurred in less than 21  year olds  the um  the sex was 48 male  most were reported by a health care  provider  and occurred in the united states 73  percent were deemed serious the most  common occurrence of the average event  was the respiratory system and there  were 14 deaths  so let's look at the event  so the most frequently reported events  on delta 8 for dyspnea followed by  toxicity the various agents  and then drug interactions and then  respiratory disorder cough you'll see  chest pain drug abuse nausea hyrexia  vomiting  and so on  now the respiratory events there's a  whole host of those as well and they're  listed here not meant for you to really  look at them but there's 79 of them so  greater than 50 of the adverse events  reported or respiratory in nature  so now i'm going to show you like i said  earlier i showed you data up to december  31st 2020 and i've done subsequent  queries where i've looked at the data  that's been presented or published for  march 31st 2021 and then again in june  of uh june 30th 2021 and there's a a  small increase in cases over time some  may say well as soon as the march cases  started to  up from the poison control start to to  get some national news you know we start  to see an increase in cases i'm not so  sure because the health  the health advisory never came out until  september of 2021  but again you'll see and so you see here  there's these 14 cases in less than 17  and it shifts there's subsequent data  that may get submitted to the fda and  then they just change the case there are  no duplicates in this to the best of my  knowledge we applied and looked for the  duplications  and then there's 13 now  so when we look at the outcomes  you know the deaths still there's you  know an increase in the number of deaths  related to this where it's a suspect  drug it doesn't say if this is it only  although there are a number of cases  where they just list delta eight where  others they have other  products as well  uh hospitalizations are in the you know  greater than 25 percent continually  serious which means a serious event  means caused the hospitalization it was  life-threatening  that type of an event  is deemed serious non-serious is  something that did not require any sort  of major intervention and it wasn't  serious or life-threatening  and that's about the in the 25 as well  and then respiratory events you know  like i had said is about 50  of the cases all have respiratory events  and the most common is dyspnea followed  by by seizure  so this is the timeline of  our delta eight investigation i'd just  like to take a moment and go through  this so you can see how i've been  monitoring this so  one year ago the first delta eight cases  were in the news from poison control  centers and i looked at this and this is  the 135 cases that i presented today  after i i did this i submitted a freedom  of information  to  the fda asking for the details on all  135 cases  so what's interesting here is is that  when i submitted it i got a phone call  from the fda  and they said that unfortunately they  could only give me 10 cases at a time  and it would take them several weeks to  process that  and over time it might take somewhere  around nine months for me to get the  case details on 135 cases  so i had a conversation with this person  and said i don't understand when i  worked in pharma if we thought that  there was a signal with a product that  we have on the market and we went and  asked for the details of those cases we  would have those cases within you know  two days usually i mean very short time  frame so it was interesting how i was  not able to get a lot of the details you  know  right away  so then um at the same time i presented  very early data  in august of last year  we requested an fda listening session  because someone at the fda actually  heard this presentation from me and made  the suggestion that i contact the fda  so we did i contacted the fda they  granted me an october first listening  session but two weeks before that  listening session the fda and the cdc  issued health warnings and remember i  told you this is my birthday so it was a  great birthday present i was very happy  but it did not receive the national news  that i had hoped that it would get  so here we are um we also continue to  present at different departments of  health  dr marku  works with the health departments in  pennsylvania and delaware and so we  presented the data there  there's been subsequent data extractions  i submitted a manuscript it's on  different a little bit different  analysis than what's published here so  slightly different  um but we launched something called the  buds info adverse event tool and that  was the  qr code early on  and here i am one year later speaking to  you about  the  delta eight poisonings so the theory is  is that in 2018 the hemp farm bill  was  put into place there was an  overproduction of cbd  prices fell and so now derivatives were  made and there that we don't believe  that  for me the delta eight itself  may actually have therapeutic properties  but we don't know if it's the  adulterants or the byproducts that are  actually causing these adverse events so  in many ways it's giving it a bad name  but we'll never know because we'll never  have the opportunity to study it and i  think for me that's probably a major  takeaway that it's unfortunate  that  that  a group  decided to push out something  that may not be so great for for  consumption  okay so the strengths and limitations  the fares data source is limited and  under reported we know that across the  board it doesn't even for for products  that are marketed  uh it's the only known national database  that i know that collects data on  safety  uh for any product specifically cannabis  or derived products  um  events are reported  there's overall exposures unknowns you  have no idea  it's really a case analysis  and signal detection tools  do not imply causation so i'm not saying  that that  delta a caused these adverse events i'm  just saying that there's a potential  signal that one should be aware of  when using certain delta eight products  especially if it's in the unregulated  market  the strength is it's an early  signal detection tool  and an analysis of proportional  reporting ratios have been used as a  measure of association in the past  so in summary there's an increase in  delta 8 thc cases mostly young males in  in the united states have had severe  reactions  these events disproportionately occur in  respiratory events compared to  cannabidiol and cannabis sativa  the fares may be the only source for  national level data right now on  cannabis derived products  so we have some recommendations so  clarification of existing regulations  would be really important because it  seems that many people are using the 0.3  thc just as delta 9 and not  total  informing consumers on the ability to  report adverse effects using the  medwatch form 3500 anyone watching this  and that could be not just cannabis it  could be  anything that you give a patient or if  you  use yourself you can self-report  something  education on products especially label  comprehension is very important have a  look at that certificate of analysis  people think that they're buying cbd  when actually there's delta 8 in the cbd  and they're having an unwanted effect  patient and consumer perspectives are  important i've done some work around  this area where it's not just listing  the adverse event but you know how  debilitating was that adverse event it's  important to really understand their  perspective  a potential to create a sentinel-like  system we've spoken to the fda about  this  a national safety reporting mechanism  for consumers is needed so we have  actually launched something it's called  you can find it at budsinfo.com  it's our beginnings of of trying to  collect cannabis derived  address event data so it doesn't matter  whether it's hemp or cbd or  or cannabis it doesn't matter and we are  seeking partnerships and collaborations  so this is the the website you know by  buds for buds i i and this is the qr  code is coming up next  so scan it now  keep it in your back pocket if you ever  have a patient who may have had unwanted  experience or the patient or consumer  can  complete it themselves  i'd like to thank john simon who's a  co-author and matthew webwire both mph  students at drexel  for  helping me create the form  and getting the qr code launched so  it was great to have them on board with  this project  so thank you  you can contact  me or dr marku  or actually john me or andres the prc  child  and jehovah marcou at marco enterprises  so thank you  you
 

